<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Punakha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Punakha is a town in western Bhutan where the Pho Chhu and Mo Chhu rivers meet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a> built its great fortress-monastery, Punakha Dzong, in 1637 to 1638, and the town was the capital of Bhutan until the 1950s. In 1907 an assembly at Punakha elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a> the first hereditary king, and important royal ceremonies are still held there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/punakha.jpg" alt="Punakha" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Technopilgrim at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PunakhaDzong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Punakha is the administrative centre of Punakha dzongkhag, one of the 20 districts of Bhutan. It lies at an elevation of 1,200 metres above sea level, about 72 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a>, a journey of about three hours by car. Unlike Thimphu, it is quite warm in winter and hot in summer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> is the main crop along the valleys of the Pho Chhu and Mo Chhu, the two main rivers of Bhutan, and Dzongkha is widely spoken in the district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Punakha was the capital of Bhutan and the seat of government until 1955, when the capital was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a>. Its great fortress, Pungthang Dewachen Phodrang (Palace of Great Happiness), better known as Punakha Dzong, was constructed under the command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal</a> and is believed to have taken about two years to complete. It is the winter residence of Bhutan's Central Monastic Body, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a>, and houses the most sacred relics of the Southern Drukpa Kagyu school, including the Rangjung Kasarpani, and the sacred remains of Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal and Tertön Padma Lingpa.</p><p>In 1907 the dzong was the site of the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a> as the first King of Bhutan. Three years later a treaty was signed at Punakha by which the British agreed not to interfere in Bhutanese internal affairs, while Bhutan allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to direct its foreign affairs. The dzong has suffered repeatedly. It was partially destroyed by fire in 1780, 1789, 1802, 1831, 1849 and 1986, and it experienced an earthquake in 1897 and a flood in 1994. Standing at the confluence of the two rivers, it is vulnerable to flash floods caused by glacier lakes, and flood damage is recorded in 1957, 1960 and 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A covered wooden cantilever bridge over the Mo Chhu was built together with the dzong in the 17th century and was washed away by a flash flood in 1957 or 1958. A new bridge of traditional construction, with a free span of 55 metres, was begun in 2006 and completed in 2008 with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The valley is known in Bhutan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming, both red and white, and the village of Ritsha, with its two-storey houses of pounded mud on stone foundations, is often called the rice bowl of Bhutan. Punakha Domchoe, held in the first month of the Bhutanese year, is an important festival combining ritual with a display of horsemanship and sword play.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Punakha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In western Bhutan he built fortress-monasteries, or dzongs, including Simtokha and <b>Punakha</b>, repelled Tibetan invasions and unified the valleys, taking the title Zhabdrung.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Zhabdrung Bhutan</a></li></ul></section>
